<!--
doc/src/sgml/ref/cypher_delete.sgml
PostgreSQL documentation
-->

<refentry id="cypher-delete">
 <indexterm zone="cypher-delete">
  <primary>DELETE</primary>
 </indexterm>

 <refmeta>
  <refentrytitle>DELETE</refentrytitle>
  <manvolnum>7</manvolnum>
  <refmiscinfo>Cypher - Language Statements</refmiscinfo>
 </refmeta>

 <refnamediv>
  <refname>DELETE</refname>
  <refpurpose>remove vertices and edges from the graph</refpurpose>
 </refnamediv>

 <refsynopsisdiv>
<synopsis>
[ DETACH ] DELETE <replaceable class="parameter">expression</replaceable> [, <replaceable class="parameter">expression</replaceable> ] ...
</synopsis>
 </refsynopsisdiv>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-delete-description">
  <title>Description</title>

  <para>
   <command>DELETE</command> removes the graph elements its expressions evaluate to. It needs
   them bound first, so it always follows a clause that found them.
  </para>

  <para>
   A plain <command>DELETE</command> refuses a vertex that still has edges. Deleting it would
   leave edges pointing at nothing, so the clause raises an error and the transaction rolls
   back. <literal>DETACH DELETE</literal> deletes the vertex's edges first and then the vertex.
   Use it whenever the vertex might be connected.
  </para>

  <para>
   Deleting an edge never needs <literal>DETACH</literal>, and never touches its endpoints.
   Several expressions may be listed. They are deleted in the order given, so listing an edge
   before its endpoints works without <literal>DETACH</literal>.
  </para>

  <para>
   <command>DELETE</command> runs once per incoming row. An element already deleted by an
   earlier row is not an error.
  </para>

  <para>
   Each expression has to be a vertex, an edge or a path. A property reference is not accepted.
   To remove a property, use <xref linkend="cypher-remove"/>.
  </para>

  <para>
   <command>DELETE</command> may end a query. Add a <xref linkend="cypher-return"/> to see what
   went.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-delete-parameters">
  <title>Parameters</title>

  <variablelist>
   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>DETACH</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Delete each vertex's connected edges along with it.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">expression</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      An expression yielding a vertex, an edge or a path, usually a variable bound by a
      <xref linkend="cypher-match"/>.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
  </variablelist>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-delete-examples">
  <title>Examples</title>

  <para>
   These examples use the graph in <xref linkend="agensgraph-example-data"/>.
  </para>

  <para>
   Build a subgraph to remove:
<programlisting>
CREATE (:movie {title: 'Tenet', year: 2020, genre: 'Action'});
MATCH (d:person {name: 'Christopher Nolan'}), (m:movie {title: 'Tenet'})
CREATE (d)-[:directed]-&gt;(m);
MATCH (m:movie {title: 'Tenet'})
CREATE (a:person {name: 'Elizabeth Debicki', born: 1990})-[:acted_in {role: 'Kat'}]-&gt;(m);

UPDATE 1
UPDATE 1
UPDATE 2
</programlisting>
   <literal>Tenet</literal> now has two edges:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie {title: 'Tenet'})-[r]-(x)
RETURN label(r) AS edge, count(*) AS n
ORDER BY edge;

    edge    | n
------------+---
 "acted_in" | 1
 "directed" | 1
(2 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   A plain <command>DELETE</command> refuses that vertex:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie {title: 'Tenet'}) DELETE m;

ERROR:  Vertex with connected edges cannot be removed
HINT:  Use DETACH DELETE or remove connected edges before deleting the vertex.
</programlisting>
   Nothing is deleted. The graph never holds an edge whose endpoint is gone.
  </para>

  <para>
   An edge needs no <literal>DETACH</literal>, and its endpoints survive:
<programlisting>
MATCH (:person {name: 'Elizabeth Debicki'})-[r:acted_in]-&gt;(:movie {title: 'Tenet'})
DELETE r;

UPDATE 1
</programlisting>
   With no edges left, a plain <command>DELETE</command> succeeds on the actor. A
   <command>RETURN</command> after it reports the element that was removed:
<programlisting>
MATCH (p:person {name: 'Elizabeth Debicki'})
DELETE p
RETURN properties(p) AS removed;

                   removed
---------------------------------------------
 {"born": 1990, "name": "Elizabeth Debicki"}
(1 row)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   <literal>Tenet</literal> still has the <literal>directed</literal> edge.
   <literal>DETACH DELETE</literal> takes both, and the tag counts the two elements:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie {title: 'Tenet'}) DETACH DELETE m;

UPDATE 2
</programlisting>
   Christopher Nolan is untouched and keeps his other edges:
<programlisting>
MATCH (p:person {name: 'Christopher Nolan'})-[:directed]-&gt;(m:movie)
RETURN m.title AS film
ORDER BY film;

      film
----------------
 "Inception"
 "Interstellar"
(2 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   List the edge before the vertices it joins and no <literal>DETACH</literal> is needed,
   because by the time the vertices are deleted the edge is gone:
<programlisting>
CREATE (:person {name: 'Extra Two'});
MATCH (a:person {name: 'Extra Two'}) CREATE (a)-[:acted_in]-&gt;(:movie {title: 'Scratch'});

UPDATE 1
UPDATE 2
</programlisting>
<programlisting>
MATCH (a:person {name: 'Extra Two'})-[r:acted_in]-&gt;(m:movie {title: 'Scratch'})
DELETE r, a, m;

UPDATE 3
</programlisting>
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-delete-notes">
  <title>Notes</title>

  <para>
   <command>DELETE</command> takes elements, not properties. Naming a property is refused
   rather than quietly ignored:
<programlisting>
MATCH (m:movie {title: 'The Matrix'}) DELETE m.title;

ERROR:  node, relationship, or path is expected
LINE 1: MATCH (m:movie {title: 'The Matrix'}) DELETE m.title;
                                                     ^
</programlisting>
   Use <xref linkend="cypher-remove"/> for a property, or <literal>SET m.title = NULL</literal>.
  </para>

  <para>
   <literal>DETACH DELETE</literal> on a busy vertex can delete a great many edges in one
   statement, and all of it is one transaction. Count first, with
   <literal>MATCH (v)-[r]-() RETURN count(r)</literal>, when you are not sure how large the
   effect will be.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-delete-compatibility">
  <title>Compatibility</title>

  <para>
   <command>DELETE</command> implements the GQL delete statement of ISO/IEC 39075:2024 section
   13.5. The standard writes it <literal>[ DETACH | NODETACH ] DELETE</literal>, with
   <literal>NODETACH</literal> the implicit default. AgensGraph implements that default but not
   the keyword: <literal>NODETACH DELETE m</literal> is a syntax error. Write a plain
   <command>DELETE</command>, which means the same thing.
  </para>

  <para>
   openCypher spells the clause the same way, with the same <literal>DETACH</literal> keyword
   and the same refusal to strand an edge, and AgensGraph accepts it unchanged.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

